{
  "image_name": "8526411f86ad056a17dceeba5f504835.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the basic idea of photoemission spectroscopy, especially angle-resolved photoemission (ARPES), and how emitted electrons are related to a material’s electronic structure. On the left, incoming light with energy \\(h\\nu\\) strikes a flat sample and ejects electrons that are collected by an electron analyser. The electron’s emission direction is defined by angles, and its kinetic energy is measured. The momentum sketches emphasize the key ARPES relation: the component of electron momentum parallel to the sample surface is conserved when the electron exits the solid, while the perpendicular component is not directly conserved because of the surface boundary.\n\nOn the right, an energy-level diagram connects the measured kinetic energy in vacuum to the electron’s original binding energy inside the sample. It shows occupied valence-band states near the Fermi level and deeper core levels, along with the vacuum level and the work function \\(\\Phi\\). Red arrows indicate that photons can excite electrons from either valence or core states into vacuum, with the final kinetic energy depending on the photon energy minus the binding energy and work function. Overall, the figure explains how ARPES converts measured electron angles and energies into information about band structure and electronic states.",
